{
	title: 'Literal !!START blocks: tool arguments need no quoting or escaping',
	spec: "Every tool argument that carries text (BASH commands and scripts, EVAL code, regexes, GREP patterns, SEND messages, COMPACT summaries, file contents) reaches the tool byte-exact as written; nothing needs quoting or escaping. The advertised form is a header line, TOOL [/* purpose */] [short arguments] [{ options }], followed by one raw block: a !!START line, literal lines, a !!END line. The block fills the tool's main text field (task hq6). Block content excludes the newline before !!END and keeps every other character, leading spaces and blank lines. A content line that is itself a marker takes one extra ! (!!!END writes a literal !!END), as in EDIT. Command splitting never splits inside a block, for any tool; this replaces EVAL's code-to-the-end rule (task v99). Quoted string literals stay valid for one-liners. EDIT keeps its own !!OLD/!!NEW/!!END grammar and is the only tool with several blocks. Quirks (task hq6) accept !!NEW in place of !!START for a single block of another tool; !!OLD outside EDIT is rejected. SYSTEM.md drops its JS-string-literal and backslash rule and teaches: put an argument that spans lines or contains quotes or backslashes in a block. Malformed framing (an unclosed block, a block for a tool without a main text field, two blocks) fails as a tool error showing the raw text; nothing runs.",
	status: 'planned',
	notes: [
		'User, 2026-11-16: every frontier model breaks JS string escaping in tool calls. Seen with Claude Fable 5.2 (high effort): a heredoc inside a BASH string turned \\n into a real newline; a rewrite then lost backslashes, breaking \\d in a regex. Models burn turns fixing escapes instead of working. The agent writing this task hit it too: tsk add through BASH doubled every backslash.',
